# BEAM on the edge

## About this notebook

This Livebook runs in a Cloudflare Worker. The BEAM VM (ERTS) is WebAssembly, and it runs in one Durable Object. There is no server, no container and no VPS.

Each cell runs in the VM of Livebook itself (the embedded runtime). The cells show the parts of Erlang and Elixir that make the BEAM different: many small processes, supervisors that restart them, messages, and code that changes while it runs.

Read these limits before you start:

* The VM has 128 MB of memory and one scheduler. A cell that does not stop blocks the other cells.
* `Mix.install/2` is not available. Kino and the dependencies of Livebook are in the release.
* The Durable Object keeps the files of `/data` (your notebooks) in its SQLite storage. The processes and their memory stop when the object stops.
* This instance has a time limit. At the limit, it stops, and its files are deleted. The next cell shows the minutes that are left.

Run the cells in order. To run all the cells, press <kbd>Esc</kbd>, then <kbd>e</kbd> and <kbd>a</kbd> (evaluate all outdated cells).

## Where this runs

```elixir
{up, _} = :erlang.statistics(:wall_clock)

%{
  system: to_string(:erlang.system_info(:system_architecture)),
  otp: to_string(:erlang.system_info(:otp_release)),
  elixir: System.version(),
  schedulers: :erlang.system_info(:schedulers),
  word_size: :erlang.system_info(:wordsize),
  processes: length(Process.list()),
  modules_loaded: length(:code.all_loaded()),
  uptime_seconds: div(up, 1000),
  instance_minutes_left:
    case System.get_env("BEAM_INSTANCE_EXPIRES") do
      nil -> nil
      expires -> div(String.to_integer(expires) - System.os_time(:second), 60)
    end
}
```

The system is `wasm32-unknown-emscripten`: the same ERTS as on a server, compiled to WebAssembly. The word size is 4 bytes, so a process takes half the memory of a process on a 64-bit server.

### A clock for the edge

On Cloudflare, the clocks of a Worker give the time of the last I/O (a protection against timing attacks). They do not move while code runs, and a timer moves them only by its own time. So a cell cannot measure its time there: `:timer.tc/1` gives 0 ms.

`Edge.measure/1` gives two numbers. `ms` is the time, which is correct on your computer and near 0 on Cloudflare. `reductions` counts the units of work of the VM, and it needs no clock. Code that a cell evaluates (not the code of a module) runs in the interpreter of Erlang terms, so it takes more reductions.

```elixir
defmodule Edge do
  @doc false
  def measure(fun) do
    {r0, _} = :erlang.statistics(:exact_reductions)
    t0 = System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)
    result = fun.()
    t1 = System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)
    {r1, _} = :erlang.statistics(:exact_reductions)
    {%{ms: Float.round((t1 - t0) / 1_000, 1), reductions: r1 - r0}, result}
  end
end

Edge.measure(fn -> Enum.reduce(1..1_000_000, 0, &+/2) end)
```

## Many small processes

A BEAM process is not a thread of the operating system. It has its own small heap, and the VM switches between processes itself. Change the number, and run the next cell again.

```elixir
count_input = Kino.Input.number("Processes", default: 10_000)
```

```elixir
count = Kino.Input.read(count_input)

{cost, pids} =
  Edge.measure(fn ->
    for _ <- 1..count do
      spawn(fn ->
        receive do
          :stop -> :ok
        end
      end)
    end
  end)

bytes =
  pids
  |> Enum.map(fn pid -> Process.info(pid, :memory) end)
  |> Enum.map(fn {:memory, m} -> m end)
  |> Enum.sum()

Enum.each(pids, &send(&1, :stop))

"#{count} processes in #{cost.ms} ms (#{cost.reductions} reductions), #{div(bytes, count)} bytes each"
```

## Messages

Processes share no memory. They send messages. This ring sends one message around 1,000 processes, 100 times: 100,000 messages.

```elixir
defmodule Edge.Ring do
  def start(size) do
    Enum.reduce(1..size, self(), fn _, next ->
      spawn(fn -> relay(next) end)
    end)
  end

  defp relay(next) do
    receive do
      :stop ->
        send(next, :stop)

      n ->
        send(next, n)
        relay(next)
    end
  end

  def run(first, rounds) do
    for n <- 1..rounds do
      send(first, n)

      receive do
        ^n -> :ok
      end
    end

    send(first, :stop)

    receive do
      :stop -> :ok
    end
  end
end

first = Edge.Ring.start(1_000)
{cost, :ok} = Edge.measure(fn -> Edge.Ring.run(first, 100) end)
"100,000 messages in #{cost.ms} ms (#{cost.reductions} reductions)"
```

Kino can draw the messages between processes. This cell traces one request and its reply:

```elixir
Kino.Process.render_seq_trace(fn ->
  parent = self()

  server =
    spawn(fn ->
      receive do
        {:ping, from} -> send(from, :pong)
      end
    end)

  send(server, {:ping, parent})

  receive do
    :pong -> :pong
  end
end)
```

## Let it crash

A supervisor starts processes and starts them again when they stop. The code of a process does not need to handle each error: it stops, and its supervisor starts a new one from a known state.

```elixir
children =
  for n <- 1..3 do
    Supervisor.child_spec({Agent, fn -> 0 end}, id: :"counter_#{n}")
  end

{:ok, sup} =
  Kino.start_child(%{
    id: :edge_supervisor,
    type: :supervisor,
    start: {Supervisor, :start_link, [children, [strategy: :one_for_one]]}
  })

Kino.Process.render_sup_tree(sup)
```

Now stop one of the counters. Look at the process ids before and after:

```elixir
before = for {id, pid, _, _} <- Supervisor.which_children(sup), do: {id, pid}
{id, victim} = Enum.random(before)
Process.exit(victim, :kill)

# The supervisor gets the exit signal and starts a new counter.
Process.sleep(50)
now = for {id, pid, _, _} <- Supervisor.which_children(sup), do: {id, pid}

%{stopped: {id, victim}, before: before, after: now}
```

## Change the code while it runs

The BEAM can load a new version of a module while processes run the old one. A process moves to the new version at its next call through the module name (`__MODULE__.loop/1`). A release upgrade uses the same mechanism.

```elixir
defmodule Edge.Greeter do
  def loop(n) do
    receive do
      {:hello, from} ->
        send(from, {:reply, "version 1, call #{n}"})
        __MODULE__.loop(n + 1)
    end
  end
end

greeter = spawn(fn -> Edge.Greeter.loop(1) end)

ask = fn ->
  send(greeter, {:hello, self()})

  receive do
    {:reply, reply} -> reply
  end
end

{greeter, ask.(), ask.()}
```

This cell compiles version 2 of the module, and asks the same process (the same process id) two times:

* The first answer comes from version 1: the process waits in the `receive` of the old code.
* Its call to `__MODULE__.loop/1` goes to version 2, so the second answer comes from the new code.
* The process keeps its state (the call count) through the change.

```elixir
Code.compile_string("""
defmodule Edge.Greeter do
  def loop(n) do
    receive do
      {:hello, from} ->
        send(from, {:reply, "version 2, call \#{n}"})
        __MODULE__.loop(n + 1)
    end
  end
end
""")

{greeter, Process.alive?(greeter), ask.(), ask.()}
```

## A live view of the VM

Kino updates the output of a cell while the cell runs. This table shows the 10 processes with the most work (reductions), and it changes each second.

```elixir
frame = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()

Kino.listen(1_000, fn _ ->
  rows =
    Process.list()
    |> Enum.map(fn pid ->
      {pid, Process.info(pid, [:registered_name, :reductions, :memory, :message_queue_len])}
    end)
    |> Enum.reject(fn {_, info} -> info == nil end)
    |> Enum.sort_by(fn {_, info} -> info[:reductions] end, :desc)
    |> Enum.take(10)
    |> Enum.map(fn {pid, info} ->
      name = if info[:registered_name] == [], do: "", else: "`#{inspect(info[:registered_name])}`"
      "| `#{inspect(pid)}` | #{name} | #{info[:reductions]} | #{info[:memory]} | #{info[:message_queue_len]} |"
    end)

  table =
    Enum.join(
      ["| Process | Name | Reductions | Memory (bytes) | Queue |", "|---|---|--:|--:|--:|" | rows],
      "\n"
    )

  Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new(table))
end)
```

## Out to the internet

The Worker opens TCP connections for the VM, and the VM does TLS itself, with the root certificates of the release. `Req` is a dependency of Livebook:

```elixir
%{status: status, body: body} = Req.get!("https://hex.pm/api/packages/kino")
{status, Map.take(body, ["name", "latest_stable_version", "html_url"])}
```

## How this works

* The Worker gets the release of Livebook (`release.bin`) from a second Worker, and starts ERTS in WebAssembly with JSPI.
* The first start of a new version makes a snapshot of the memory of the VM, after it loaded the modules of the boot and before it started Livebook. The next starts restore that snapshot, and then start Livebook.
* The Durable Object writes the files of `/data` to its SQLite storage when the VM closes them. A new VM gets them back before it starts. So your notebooks stay after the object stops.
* Each visitor starts an instance: its own Durable Object, VM, files and storage, at a random path (`/t/NAME`). A registry object limits the instances at one time, keeps a queue, and gives each instance its time limit.
* Kino draws its diagrams in an iframe of a second site (a third Worker), apart from the pages of Livebook.
